  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe upper level low that helped generate wide spread \nthunderstorms with heavy rain on Monday, will continue to slowly \ndrift to the west. The threat for heavy rain on Tuesday afternoon \nwill be reduced, we will still have plenty of moisture in the \natmosphere, but the better dynamics will begin to push into \nArizona. High temperatures will continue to run 5 to 10 degrees \nbelow average. Enjoy the \"cooler\" weather, since it won't last \nlong.\n\nOn Wednesday the upper level ridge to our northeast will begin to\nbuild into New Mexico, which will push most of the moisture into \nArizona and start to really limit our thunderstorm coverage. High \ntemperatures on Wednesday will be near or a degree or two below \naverage. For Thursday and Friday, the upper level ridge will slide\ninto New Mexico and we will see high temperature creep 5 to 10 \ndegrees above average. Many lowland locations will see triple \ndigit heat on Friday (if not on Thursday). For the weekend, the \nupper level ridge drifts a little further north. This will allow a\nlittle moisture to bring some thunderstorm chances mainly in area\nmountains and to keep most lowland locations high temperatures \nabove the triple digit mark. For the start of next week, the exact\nlocation of the upper level ridge is still in doubt, but no \nmatter what, things still look pretty hot with just a slight \nchance for rain.",
